me","H8064":"heaven","H1523":"to rejoice","H6476":"to break out","H7440":"cry","H5162":"comfort","H6041":"afflicted","H6726":"Zion","H5800":"forsake","H136":"Lord","H7911":"to forget","H802":"woman","H5764":"infant","H1121":"child","H1571":"also","H595":"I","H2005":"look!","H3709":"palm","H2710":"to decree","H2346":"wall","H5048":"before","H8548":"continually","H4116":"to hasten","H2040":"to overthrow","H2717":"to dry","H4480":"from","H5375":"raise","H5439":"around","H6908":"to gather","H2416":"alive","H5002":"utterance","H5716":"ornament","H3847":"to clothe","H7194":"to conspire","H3618":"bride","H2723":"desolation","H2035":"ruins","H3334":"be distressed","H3427":"to dwell","H7368":"to remove","H1104":"to swallow up","H5750":"still","H241":"ear","H7923":"bereavement","H6862":"narrow","H4725":"place","H5066":"approach","H3824":"heart","H4310":"who?","H3205":"to beget","H7921":"be bereaved","H1565":"solitary","H5493":"remove","H1431":"to magnify","H7604":"to remain","H905":"pole","H375":"where?","H1992":"they(masc.)","H3069":"YHWH/God","H5251":"Banner","H2684":"bosom","H1323":"daughter","H3802":"shoulder","H8282":"princess","H3243":"to suckle","H639":"anger","H6083":"dust","H7272":"foot","H3897":"to lick","H3045":"to know","H954":"be ashamed","H6960":"to await","H3947":"take","H1368":"mighty man","H4455":"prey","H518":"if","H7628":"captivity","H6662":"righteous","H4422":"to escape","H6184":"ruthless","H3401":"opponent","H7378":"to contend","H3467":"to save","H398":"to eat","H3238":"to oppress","H1320":"flesh","H6071":"sweet","H1818":"blood","H7937":"be drunk","H46":"mighty"},"ccc":{"1":[713],"2":[713],"3":[713],"4":[713],"5":[64,713],"6":[64,713],"13":[716],"14":[219,370],"15":[219,239,370]},"cccp":{"64":"__PG#","219":"__P16#","239":"__P17#","370":"__P1B#","713":"__P22#","716":"__P22#"},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Give. This fresh discourse continues to chap. lvi. 9., relating to the Messias, who is introduced speaking to all the world, Acts xiii. 47., and 2 Corinthians vi. 2. Some apply a part to Cyrus, Isaias, or John the Baptist, as to his figures. (Calmet) — The prophet has foretold the conversion of the Gentiles, as he now does, like an evangelist. (St. Jerome) — Many Jews will embrace the gospel at the end of time. (Worthington)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Sword, penetrating the very soul, (Hebrews iv. 12.) and slaying the wicked, Apocalypse i. 16., and 2 Thessalonians ii. 8. Without Christ, his ministers can apply this sword to little purpose. Cyrus cut asunder the bonds of the captives by his decree. (Calmet) — Grotius improperly explains all of Isaias. (Houbigant)"],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Glory. In the Church, God is adored in spirit and truth. Isaias is ordered thus to address the Israelites."],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","God. He will reward the labours of his ministers, though the people be obstinate. The prophets and our Saviour frequently complain, chap. lxv. 2., and Mark ix. 18."],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Will not. Hebrew, Septuagint, &c., “to be gathered.” (Haydock) — The original text, independent of the Masorets, may have the sense of the Vulgate. Yet there are other proofs of the synagogue’s rejection. (Calmet) — Protestants, “though Israel be not gathered, yet,” &c. Marginal note, or “that Israel may be gathered, and I may,” &c. (Haydock)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Earth. St. Paul, explains this of Christ, (Acts xiii.) who said, Go teach all nations, Matthew xxviii. Isaias was one of his first preachers."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Despised, Christ, Cyrus, or Isaias; though it refer chiefly to our Saviour."],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Ways. The captives shall find every convenience. (Calmet)"],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","South. Hebrew Sinim; (Haydock) China, (St. Jerome) or rather Sin, or Pelusium, and Sinai, in Egypt and Arabia. Septuagint, “Persians.” (Calmet)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Sion, the Jews, who will at last be converted in great numbers. (Houbigant)"],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Hands, which were nailed to the cross. Septuagint, “I have delineated thy walls on my hands, and thou art before me always.” (Haydock) — The Assyrians wore such characters on their hands or necks. (Lucian) (Leviticus xix. 28.) (Calmet) — Christ will always love his Church, which is of all times and nations. (Worthington)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Of thee. Sanballat, &c., shall yield to Zorobabel, Nehemias, &c."],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","Inhabitants. The country was better peopled, (Calmet) and Jerusalem enlarged under the Machabees. (Josephus, Jewish Wars v. 6.)"],["haydock",20,null,"haydock","Barrenness, of which thou complainest. He alludes to the captives who returned, and to Christian converts. (Menochius)"],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Shoulders. Thus the Syrians commonly carried children astride. (Cotovic. xiv.)"],["haydock",23,null,"haydock","Nurses. The Persian kings favoured the captives. The greatest monarchs bow before the prelates of the Church, (Menochius) and kiss the Pope’s toe. They venerate relics, (Haydock) and greatly enrich the Church. (Calmet)"],["haydock",24,null,"haydock","Strong. Can I force the Babylonians to yield? Surely, and the devil also, Luke xi. 21. — Mighty. Hebrew, “just,” in lawful war. Septuagint, “unjustly.”"],["haydock",26,null,"haydock","Flesh. They shall attack one another. (Calmet) — Neriglissor slew Evil-merodac. (Berosus, apud Josephus, contra Apion i.) — Gobrias and Gadatas betrayed and killed Baltassar. (Xenophon iv. 5, 7.) (Calmet) — In their fury they shall tear their own flesh. (Menochius)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]},"fa":[[6,null,0,"Exposition of the Christian Faith","Book 5",0,397,"… Not only does he undergo service in the character of man by reason of his descent from David, but also by reason of his name, as it is written: \"I have found David my servant\";4 and elsewhere: \"Behold, I will send to you my Servant, the Orient is his name. And the Son himself says, \"Thus says the Lord, that formed me from the womb to be his servant and said to me: It is a great thing for you to be called my servant. Behold, I have set you up for a witness to my people and a light to the Gentiles, that you may be for salvation to the ends of the earth. …"],[14,null,1,"Morals on the Book of Job","Book32",1,604,"And again speaking by the comparison of a wife, He says: \"Even if she shall have forgotten, yet will I not forget thee. For who can be ignorant, that the memory of God is neither broken off by oblivion, nor yet repaired by recollection? But when He neglects and passes over some things, He is said, after the manner of minds, to forget, and when, after a long time, He visits the things He wills, He is said, after the fashion of our changeableness, to have remembered. For how does oblivion weaken the strength of that Godhead, with Which even praiseworthy memory itself has no essential agreement. For men remember no things, except those which are either past or absent. …"],[15,null,2,"Conferences of John Cassian - Part II","13. The Third Conference of Abbot Chaeremon",2,435,"This providence and love of God therefore, which the Lord in His unwearied goodness vouchsafes to show us, He compares to the tenderest heart of a kind mother, as He wishes to express it by a figure of human affection, and finds in His creatures no such feeling of love, to which he could better compare it. And He uses this example, because nothing dearer can be found in human nature, saying: \"Can a mother forget her child, that she should not have compassion on the son of her womb?\" But not content with this comparison He at once goes beyond it, and subjoins these words: \"And though she may forget, yet will not I forget thee.\""],[18,null,1,"Morals on the Book of Job","Book27",1,604,"We have often said already that blessed Job stands for a type of Holy Church. But all they, who are joined to it by the unity of the Faith, are the garments of the Church. Of which the Lord says to the same Church by the Prophet, \"As I live, thou shall be clothed with all these, as with an ornament. But by the south wind, which is doubtless warm, is designated, not improperly, the Holy Spirit, for when any one is touched by It, he is freed from the torpor of his iniquity. …"]],"fau":[["ambrose","Ambrose of Milan",1,"father",null,1,397],["gregory-great","Gregory the Great",1,"father",null,1,604],["cassian","John Cassian",0,"father",null,0,435]],"ftr":[["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 10 (1896)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf210"],["Library of the Fathers, Morals on the Book of Job (1844-1850)"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 11 (1894)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf211"]]}